LONG.update({
'choose-album-background-psd':('How to Choose the Right Album Background PSD',[
"The background is the quietest part of an album spread, but it decides the mood of every page. A wrong background makes good photos look cheap; the right one ties thirty spreads together. This guide explains how to pick and use album background PSDs so they support your photos instead of competing with them.",
"## Start from the photos, not the background",
"Look at the selected photos for an event before choosing anything. Note the dominant colours (the bride's saree, the stage decor, the venue lighting) and whether the images are bright daylight shots or warm indoor ones. A haldi ceremony full of yellows needs a different background from a night reception lit in blue and purple. Pick backgrounds whose colours either repeat one of those dominant tones or stay neutral.",
"## Low detail where the faces go",
"The most useful backgrounds keep the centre calm and put texture, florals or ornaments towards the edges. Before you commit, place your hero photo and frames over the background and squint at the screen. If the background pattern still grabs your eye before the faces do, it is too busy. You can calm a busy background with a Gaussian Blur on a duplicate layer, or by adding a solid colour fill layer above it at 20 to 40 percent opacity.",
"## Match resolution to the sheet",
"A background must be at least as large as the sheet in pixels. A 12x36 inch spread at 300 DPI needs 10800 x 3600 pixels; at 200 DPI it needs 7200 x 2400. Scaling a small background up makes textures soft and patterns blurry, which is very visible on glossy photo paper. Every design on PSD Album lists its pixel size and DPI so you can check this before downloading. Browse the [album background PSD collection](/album-background-psd/) for sheets made at album proportions.",
"## Keep one family per event",
"Use the same background, or backgrounds from the same set, for all spreads of one event, then change the family when the story moves to the next event. This gives the album chapters: a warm floral set for the mehendi, a gold and maroon set for the muhurtham, a darker set for the reception. Mixing six unrelated textures inside one event is the fastest way to make an album look like a collection of separate templates.",
"## Recolour instead of searching forever",
"If you like a background's pattern but not its colour, add a Hue/Saturation adjustment layer and clip it to the background with Alt+Ctrl+G. Move the hue slider to shift the colour, and lower saturation for a more premium, muted look. A Color Balance layer is better for warming or cooling a background slightly so it matches the skin tones in your photos.",
"## Think about the fold and the edges",
"Backgrounds should run all the way into the bleed area so no white edge appears after trimming. Avoid strong lines or ornaments exactly on the centre fold; they look broken when the album is opened. If a background has a symmetrical design, check that its centre lines up with the 18 inch fold on a 12x36 sheet.",
"## Backgrounds for different album types",
"- **Wedding albums:** gold, maroon, ivory and deep green textures, temple or mandala motifs used lightly.",
"- **Pre-wedding albums:** clean, airy backgrounds, soft gradients and plenty of white space so cinematic photos breathe.",
"- **Birthday and baby albums:** pastels and playful shapes, but still calm behind faces. See the [birthday album PSD collection](/birthday-album-psd/).",
"- **Studio prints:** for portrait cut-outs use dedicated [studio background PSDs](/studio-background-psd/) rather than album textures.",
"## A quick test before printing",
"Export one spread as a small JPG and look at it on your phone. Phones exaggerate contrast and saturation, so if the background looks loud there, it will probably look loud in print too. Adjust, then print a single test sheet if the album is going to a new lab. A few minutes spent on the background decision saves an entire album reprint."]),
'clipping-masks-explained':('Clipping Masks Explained for Album Designers',[
"Almost every album PSD template depends on one Photoshop feature: the clipping mask. If you understand how it works, placing photos into frames becomes fast and predictable. If you do not, templates feel confusing and photos seem to disappear. This guide explains clipping masks in plain terms and shows the problems designers run into most often.",
"## What a clipping mask does",
"A clipping mask uses the shape of one layer (the base layer) to control which parts of the layers above it are visible. In an album template the base layer is the photo frame: a rectangle, circle, arch or brush-stroke shape. When you clip a photo to it, the photo only shows where the frame shape has pixels. Everything outside the frame is hidden but not deleted, so you can move and resize the photo freely.",
"## Creating one",
"- In the Layers panel, select the frame layer.",
"- Use File > Place Embedded to bring in your photo; it appears directly above the frame.",
"- Press Alt+Ctrl+G (Option+Cmd+G on Mac), or right-click the photo layer and choose Create Clipping Mask.",
"- The photo layer becomes indented with a small down-arrow, which means it is clipped.",
"You can also hold Alt (Option) and click on the line between the two layers in the Layers panel. To release a clipping mask, use the same shortcut again.",
"## Clipping several layers to one frame",
"You can stack more than one layer on the same base: the photo, then a Curves adjustment for brightness, then a Color Balance layer. Each one must be clipped. This is the cleanest way to adjust one photo without affecting the rest of the spread.",
"## Why my photo disappeared",
"- **Wrong order:** the photo must be directly above the frame. If it is below, clipping does nothing useful. Drag it up.",
"- **Clipped to the wrong layer:** if a text layer or ornament sits between the frame and the photo, the photo clips to that layer instead. Move it.",
"- **Empty frame layer:** some templates use a hidden or 0% fill layer as the frame. Fill opacity can be 0 and clipping still works, but if the layer itself is hidden, the clipped photo is hidden too.",
"- **Photo outside the frame area:** press Ctrl+T and drag the photo back over the frame.",
"## Clipping masks vs layer masks",
"A layer mask is painted directly on a layer in black and white to hide parts of it. A clipping mask uses another layer's shape. Templates use clipping masks because the frame shape stays fixed while you swap photos. Layer masks are better for blending a photo's edge softly into a background. You can combine both: clip the photo to the frame, then add a soft layer mask to fade one side.",
"## Smart Objects keep photos sharp",
"Place photos as Smart Objects (Place Embedded does this automatically). You can then resize them many times inside the frame without losing quality. Avoid enlarging a photo beyond 100% of its original size; if a frame is very large, choose a higher-resolution photo for it.",
"## Speed tips",
"- Record an action that places a photo, clips it and opens Free Transform.",
"- Name frame layers (Photo 1, Photo 2) so you always know which one is selected.",
"- Group each frame with its clipped layers so you can move a whole photo block at once.",
"Once clipping masks are second nature, any template from our [wedding album design PSD collection](/wedding-album-design-psd/) becomes a five-minute job per spread. For the full editing workflow see [how to edit a wedding album PSD](/blog/how-to-edit-wedding-album-psd/)."]),
'organise-photos-before-album-design':('How to Organise Photos Before Designing an Album',[
"Most of the time lost in album design is not spent in Photoshop. It is spent hunting for a photo, re-exporting images or redoing spreads because the order changed. A simple organisation routine before design saves hours on every album. Here is a practical system used by many studios.",
"## 1. Copy, back up, then cull",
"Copy all camera cards to a project folder named with the date and the couple's names, and make a second backup on another drive before deleting anything from the cards. Then cull: remove blinks, duplicates, missed focus and near-identical bursts. Lightroom, Photo Mechanic or any viewer with star ratings works. The goal is a shortlist, not a final selection.",
"## 2. Folder structure that matches the story",
"Create numbered folders in event order so the album sequence is visible on disk:",
"- 01 Bride preparation",
"- 02 Groom preparation",
"- 03 Ceremony and rituals",
"- 04 Couple portraits",
"- 05 Family groups",
"- 06 Reception",
"- 07 Details and decor",
"Numbered names keep the folders in the right order in every program. For Tamil weddings you might split rituals into Nichayathartham, Muhurtham and Oonjal; see our [Tamil wedding album page order](/blog/tamil-wedding-album-page-order/) guide.",
"## 3. Rate for the album, not for the gallery",
"Clients receive a large online gallery, but the album needs fewer images. Give five stars to hero images (one per spread), four stars to strong supporting images, and leave the rest. A 30-sheet album typically needs 30 hero images and three to five supporting images per spread. Counting stars at this stage tells you immediately whether an event has too many or too few photos for its planned spreads.",
"## 4. Colour-correct in batches",
"Correct white balance and exposure per event, using one reference image and syncing the settings to similar shots. Skin tones that change from page to page are a common complaint, and fixing them later inside layered PSDs is much slower. See [colour correction tips for album photos](/blog/album-colour-correction-tips/).",
"## 5. Export at the right size",
"Export the selected images as full-resolution JPGs in sRGB into a separate folder, with the folder number kept in the file name (03_045.jpg). Do not export small web versions for album work; you will need the pixels when a photo becomes a full-page hero. A 12x36 sheet at 300 DPI is 10800 x 3600 pixels, so even half of that sheet needs a 5400 pixel wide image to print at full quality.",
"## 6. Make a page plan",
"Before opening a template, write a one-line plan for each sheet: sheet number, event, hero photo file name and the number of supporting photos. A spreadsheet is enough. Share it with the client when the album has strict requirements (for example, must-include family members). This plan also helps you choose templates: a spread with one hero and four small frames needs a different layout from a spread with two equal portraits.",
"## 7. Choose templates last",
"With the plan ready, choose a template family that fits the photo counts and orientations. Browse by size in the [12x36 wedding album PSD collection](/12x36-wedding-album-psd-free-download/) and keep downloaded templates in a separate Templates folder, not mixed with client files.",
"## 8. Archive the whole project",
"After delivery, keep the culled originals, the exported selects, the layered PSDs, the final print JPGs and the page plan together. Reprints, parent albums and anniversary orders are much easier when everything is in one place."]),
'album-colour-correction-tips':('Colour Correction Tips for Wedding Album Photos',[
"Clients may not know the word 'colour cast', but they immediately notice when the bride's skin looks orange on one page and grey on the next. Consistent colour is one of the main differences between a professional album and an amateur one. These tips focus on practical correction for Indian wedding photos, which mix daylight, tungsten, LED stage lights and coloured decor in a single day.",
"## Fix white balance per lighting situation",
"Group photos by lighting, not only by event: daylight outdoors, indoor tungsten, mandap with LED, reception stage. Within each group, pick a reference photo with a neutral element (a white shirt, a grey wall, the white of the eyes) and set white balance with the eyedropper in Lightroom or Camera Raw. Sync that setting to the rest of the group, then fine-tune. Working from RAW files gives you far more latitude than JPGs.",
"## Prioritise skin tones",
"Faces matter more than decor. Indian skin tones vary widely, and the aim is natural warmth, not a uniform orange or a pale, desaturated look. Use the HSL panel to adjust orange and red hue and saturation gently rather than pushing overall saturation. If decor colours fight with skin, use a mask or the colour mixer to adjust only the decor.",
"## Exposure and contrast",
"Album paper, especially glossy or laminated Karizma pages, prints slightly darker than most screens show. Keep faces well exposed, lift shadows modestly and avoid crushing blacks in dark sarees and suits. Clipped highlights on white outfits look flat in print; pull highlights down until detail returns.",
"## Watch out for coloured stage lights",
"Reception stages often use magenta, blue or green LEDs. Removing the cast completely can make the photo look unnatural; the goal is believable skin with some of the stage mood left. Reduce the specific colour's saturation, correct skin with a local adjustment brush, and keep the background lighting as atmosphere.",
"## Match photos inside a spread",
"After placing photos in a template, look at the spread as a whole. Two images taken minutes apart under different lights can look mismatched side by side. In Photoshop, clip a Curves or Color Balance adjustment layer to the odd photo (Alt+Ctrl+G) and match it to its neighbours. Clipping keeps the correction limited to that photo.",
"## Calibrate your screen, and lower its brightness",
"An uncalibrated laptop at full brightness is the most common cause of dark prints. A hardware calibrator is the best investment; without one, lower screen brightness when editing for print and compare against a print you know is correct. Many labs will print a single test sheet at low cost before you commit to a full album.",
"## Export in sRGB",
"Most photo-printed albums in India are produced from sRGB JPG files. If you edit in Adobe RGB or ProPhoto, convert to sRGB when exporting album sheets. Read [RGB vs CMYK for photo albums](/blog/rgb-vs-cmyk-for-albums/) for why converting to CMYK usually hurts photo albums.",
"## Quick consistency checklist",
"- Skin tones look the same person to person and page to page.",
"- Whites are white, not blue or yellow.",
"- No page is noticeably darker than its neighbours.",
"- Decor colours are rich but not glowing or blocky.",
"- The album was proofed on a calibrated or dimmed screen.",
"Good colour work makes any template look better. Once your photos are consistent, choose layouts from the [Karizma album PSD collection](/karizma-album-psd/) and the spreads will flow naturally."]),
'album-cover-design-guide':('Album Cover Design Guide: Making a Strong First Impression',[
"The cover is the first thing a client touches and the image that appears in every photo of the album on social media. A strong cover feels simple and confident; a weak one looks like an inside page squeezed onto the front. This guide covers layout, typography and technical preparation for album covers.",
"## Choose one hero photo",
"A cover works best with a single strong image: a close couple portrait, an emotional moment or a striking silhouette. Avoid collages of six or eight photos; small images become unreadable at cover size and compete with the names. If the client insists on multiple photos, use one large image and at most two small supporting ones on the back cover.",
"## Leave space for the names",
"Plan where the names and date will sit before placing the photo. Common layouts are names centred over a darker area of the photo, names on a solid panel beside the photo, or names in a small band at the bottom. Keep the text away from faces and well inside the safe area. Our [album cover dimensions guide](/blog/album-cover-dimensions/) explains closed sizes, bleed and wrap margins.",
"## Typography that survives printing",
"- Use at most two fonts: one display or script font for names, one clean font for the date or venue.",
"- Avoid very thin scripts on textured, leather-look or canvas covers; the fine strokes disappear.",
"- Make names large enough to read from arm's length, and check the spelling with the client in writing.",
"- Gold or silver foil effects look best with simple, bold letterforms.",
"See [choosing fonts for wedding albums](/blog/fonts-for-wedding-albums/) for pairings that work well.",
"## Match the cover to the inside",
"The cover should feel like the same book as the spreads inside. Pick a colour from the opening spread for the name panel, or reuse an ornament from the template family you used inside. A royal gold album with a minimal black-and-white cover can work, but it should be a deliberate choice, not an accident.",
"## Front-only, wrap-around and acrylic covers",
"Labs offer different cover types, and each needs a different file. A printed front panel needs only the front size plus bleed. A wrap-around cover needs back, spine and front in one file with the spine width for that page count. Acrylic or glass covers often show the photo through a clear panel, so the edges of the photo may not be visible. Always download the lab's current template and follow its guides.",
"## Spine and back cover",
"Keep the spine simple: names or initials and perhaps the year, centred and readable when the album stands on a shelf. The back cover can carry a quieter photo, a small studio logo if the client agrees, or simply a matching background.",
"## Technical checklist",
"- Correct closed size for the album (12x18 for a 12x36 album).",
"- Bleed included and background extended into it.",
"- Text and faces inside the safe area.",
"- sRGB JPG or the format the lab requests, at 300 DPI if the lab allows.",
"- Fonts rasterised or embedded in the print copy.",
"## Using a cover template",
"Start with a layout from the [album cover PSD collection](/album-cover-psd/), clip your hero photo into the main frame, edit the names, then hide any ornaments that clutter the design. Simple covers usually age better than heavily decorated ones."]),
'fonts-for-wedding-albums':('Choosing Fonts for Wedding Album Designs',[
"Typography is a small part of an album, but it is often what makes it look either premium or homemade. Names on the cover, headings for events and short captions all need fonts that are readable in print and consistent across the whole book. Here is how to choose and use them.",
"## Use a pair, not a collection",
"Pick one display font and one text font for the entire album. The display font is for names and event headings (for example 'Muhurtham' or 'Reception'). The text font is for dates, venues and short captions. Using a different decorative font on every spread is the most common typography mistake; it makes the album feel like unrelated templates stitched together.",
"## Display fonts: script, serif or modern",
"- **Script fonts** suit traditional and romantic albums. Choose ones with clear letterforms and avoid extremely thin strokes; they break up on textured or laminated paper.",
"- **Elegant serif fonts** work well for royal and classic themes, especially in all capitals with generous letter spacing.",
"- **Modern sans-serif fonts** suit pre-wedding, minimal and destination albums.",
"Test a display font with the actual names. Some scripts look beautiful in samples but create awkward joins with particular letter combinations.",
"## Text fonts should be invisible",
"The text font should simply be readable: a clean sans-serif or a simple serif at a comfortable size. Captions in albums are read at arm's length; at 300 DPI, a 10 to 12 point size is usually the minimum for comfortable reading. Avoid setting long quotes in script fonts.",
"## Indian language text",
"Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam, Hindi and other scripts need fonts that support them properly. Use Unicode fonts that include the script and check that conjuncts and vowel signs render correctly in Photoshop. If a template uses a Latin font for a heading and you type in Tamil, the text may show boxes or fall back to a system font. Proofread Indian language text with someone fluent before printing.",
"## Missing fonts in downloaded templates",
"When you open a PSD that uses a font you do not have, Photoshop shows a missing-font warning and substitutes a default when you edit the layer. This is normal. Replace it with a font you own that has a similar style. Do not install fonts from untrusted sites; many 'free font' downloads come bundled with unwanted software. Google Fonts and Adobe Fonts (with a Creative Cloud subscription) are reliable sources.",
"## Placement rules",
"- Keep names and dates well inside the safe area and away from the centre fold.",
"- Place text over calm areas of the photo or background, or add a subtle shadow or panel behind it.",
"- Align text to something on the page: the edge of a frame, the centre of a panel, the line of an ornament.",
"- Keep the same heading size and position for every event title throughout the album.",
"## Colour and effects",
"Gold gradients, soft drop shadows and outer glows can help text stand out, but use them lightly. A small, soft shadow at low opacity is usually enough. Heavy bevels and multiple strokes date an album quickly.",
"## Final proofing",
"Spelling mistakes in names and dates are the most common reason for album reprints. Ask the client to approve all text in writing, check it again at 100% zoom, and rasterise or embed fonts in the final print copy so the lab cannot substitute them. Then pair your fonts with a layout from the [wedding album design PSD collection](/wedding-album-design-psd/)."]),
'speed-up-album-design-workflow':('How to Speed Up Your Album Design Workflow',[
"During the wedding season, studios often have more albums waiting than designers to finish them. Faster design is not about rushing; it is about removing repeated work so you can spend your time on photo selection and storytelling. These workflow improvements work in any studio, from a single freelancer to a team.",
"## Standardise the start of every project",
"Create a project folder template with the same sub-folders every time: Originals, Selects, PSD, Print, Client Preview. Use the same naming pattern (date, couple, event). When every project looks the same, you never waste time searching. See [how to organise photos before designing an album](/blog/organise-photos-before-album-design/) for a full structure.",
"## Approve the page plan before designing",
"A written page plan (sheet number, event, hero photo, number of supporting photos) approved by the client prevents the biggest time sink: redesigning spreads after the client changes the order. Even a simple spreadsheet works.",
"## Build your own template library",
"Downloaded templates are a starting point. When you adjust a layout and like the result, save it as your own clean template with empty frames. Over a season you will build a library of layouts that suit your style and the photo counts you usually have. Organise templates by size (12x36, 12x30, 12x18) and by frame count (1 hero + 3, 2 equal, 6 grid). Our [12x36 wedding album PSD collection](/12x36-wedding-album-psd-free-download/) is a good base.",
"## Use Photoshop actions",
"Record actions for the steps you repeat dozens of times per album:",
"- Place a photo, clip it to the selected frame and open Free Transform.",
"- Add a clipped Curves and Color Balance adjustment for matching a photo.",
"- Flatten a copy, convert to sRGB and save a print JPG with the sheet number.",
"- Save a small preview JPG for client approval.",
"Assign function keys to the actions you use most. Batch (File > Automate > Batch) can run the export action over a whole folder of finished PSDs.",
"## Learn the shortcuts",
"Shortcuts for clipping (Alt+Ctrl+G), free transform (Ctrl+T), layer selection and zoom save seconds that add up to hours. Our list of [Photoshop shortcuts for album design](/blog/photoshop-shortcuts-album-design/) covers the essential ones.",
"## Correct colour before design",
"Batch colour correction in Lightroom or Camera Raw is far faster than adjusting photos one by one inside PSDs. Correct per event and lighting group, export selects, then design. You will still fine-tune inside spreads, but only a little.",
"## Give Photoshop enough resources",
"12x36 sheets at 300 DPI are large documents. In Preferences > Performance, give Photoshop 70 to 80 percent of RAM, use a fast SSD as the scratch disk, and keep the History states moderate. Closing documents you are not using also helps. 16 GB of RAM is comfortable for album work; 8 GB will feel slow with several spreads open.",
"## Batch client previews",
"Instead of exporting previews one at a time, export all finished spreads at once as small JPGs and combine them into a single PDF or an online gallery. Collect all feedback in one round, then make every change in one session.",
"## Measure where the time goes",
"For one album, note roughly how long each stage takes: culling, colour, design, revisions, export. Most studios find that revisions and searching for files take longer than the actual design. Fix those first."]),
'photoshop-shortcuts-album-design':('25 Photoshop Shortcuts Every Album Designer Should Know',[
"Album design repeats the same actions hundreds of times: placing photos, clipping them into frames, resizing, zooming and exporting. Learning the right shortcuts is the cheapest way to finish albums faster. These are the Windows shortcuts; on a Mac use Cmd for Ctrl and Option for Alt.",
"## Placing and clipping photos",
"- **Alt+Ctrl+G** – create or release a clipping mask (clip the photo into the frame).",
"- **Ctrl+T** – Free Transform, to resize and rotate the selected photo. Press Enter to apply, Esc to cancel.",
"- **Shift while transforming** – in recent Photoshop versions, scaling keeps proportions by default; holding Shift switches to free scaling.",
"- **Alt+click between two layers** in the Layers panel – another way to clip.",
"- **Ctrl+J** – duplicate a layer or selection, useful for trying a variation.",
"## Selecting and arranging layers",
"- **Ctrl+click on the canvas** with the Move tool – select the layer under the cursor (or enable Auto-Select).",
"- **Ctrl+]** and **Ctrl+[** – move a layer up or down one position.",
"- **Ctrl+G** – group selected layers; **Shift+Ctrl+G** ungroups.",
"- **Ctrl+E** – merge down (use carefully in templates).",
"- **Alt+[** and **Alt+]** – select the layer below or above.",
"- **/ (forward slash)** – toggle Lock transparent pixels on the selected layer, handy when recolouring an ornament without painting outside it.",
"## Navigating large sheets",
"- **Ctrl+0** – fit the whole spread on screen.",
"- **Ctrl+1** – view at 100%, the only zoom where you can judge sharpness.",
"- **Space + drag** – pan around the sheet.",
"- **H + click and hold** – Bird's-eye view: zoom out temporarily, then release where you want to go.",
"- **Tab** – hide panels to see the spread cleanly; **F** cycles screen modes.",
"## Guides and alignment",
"- **Ctrl+R** – show rulers, then drag guides for the centre fold and safe area.",
"- **Ctrl+;** – show or hide guides.",
"- **Alt+Ctrl+;** – lock guides.",
"- **Arrow keys** (with Move tool) – nudge by one pixel; **Shift+Arrow** nudges by ten.",
"## Adjustments and history",
"- **Ctrl+M** – Curves; **Ctrl+U** – Hue/Saturation; **Ctrl+B** – Color Balance. For non-destructive work, add these as adjustment layers from the Layers panel and clip them.",
"- **Ctrl+Z** – undo; recent versions allow multiple undos with repeated Ctrl+Z.",
"- **Ctrl+Alt+Z** – step backward in older versions.",
"## Saving and exporting",
"- **Ctrl+S** – save the layered PSD regularly.",
"- **Alt+Ctrl+S** – Save a Copy, used for flattened print JPGs.",
"- **Alt+Shift+Ctrl+W** – Export As, for quick client previews.",
"## Make your own shortcuts",
"Edit > Keyboard Shortcuts lets you assign keys to menu commands you use often, such as Place Embedded or Convert to Profile. Actions can also be assigned to function keys (F2 to F12). A single key that places, clips and transforms a photo can save several minutes per spread.",
"## Practise on a real template",
"Shortcuts stick when you use them on real work. Download a layout from the [Karizma album PSD collection](/karizma-album-psd/) and design one spread using only the keyboard for these actions. Then read [clipping masks explained](/blog/clipping-masks-explained/) if any step felt unclear."]),
})
